WEBVTT

00:00:00.176 --> 00:00:02.789
Rotations do not live in a flat space.

00:00:02.789 --> 00:00:06.997
Inside all three by three matrices they
form a curved surface, a manifold,

00:00:06.998 --> 00:00:08.590
like the surface of a globe.

00:00:08.589 --> 00:00:12.214
So an optimizer that adds a small step
walks off the surface.

00:00:12.214 --> 00:00:17.313
A tenth of a radian added this way sends a
unit vector to length one point zero zero
five,

00:00:17.314 --> 00:00:19.977
and the determinant becomes one point zero
one.

00:00:19.976 --> 00:00:23.376
It stretches space, so it's no longer a
rotation.

00:00:23.376 --> 00:00:25.451
The exponential map fixes this:

00:00:25.451 --> 00:00:28.826
keep a three-number step, and bend it back
onto the surface.

00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:33.396
Given matched points, the best rotation
has a closed form, with no iterations.

00:00:33.396 --> 00:00:34.696
This is Kabsch.

00:00:34.696 --> 00:00:36.546
Subtract both centroids.

00:00:36.546 --> 00:00:39.471
Form the cross-covariance H and take its
SVD.

00:00:39.471 --> 00:00:42.433
The rotation is V times U transpose.

00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:47.521
If that comes out as a reflection, flip
the sign of the smallest singular
direction.

00:00:47.521 --> 00:00:52.133
This square, turned by ninety degrees,
gives back exactly that turn.

00:00:52.101 --> 00:00:53.751
Now bundle adjustment.

00:00:53.751 --> 00:00:57.263
It refines ten cameras and ten thousand
points together.

00:00:57.263 --> 00:01:00.488
Each camera has six numbers and each point
three.

00:01:00.488 --> 00:01:02.376
How big is the linear system?

00:01:02.376 --> 00:01:04.513
And could you really solve that directly?

00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:06.176
Pause and estimate.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.030
Thirty thousand and sixty unknowns.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:14.928
But each residual touches just one camera
and one point, so the system is sparse,

00:01:14.929 --> 00:01:17.831
and its point block is diagonal in small
pieces.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:21.117
Solve the points in terms of the cameras,
and substitute.

00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:23.217
What's left is the Schur complement:

00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:26.630
a sixty by sixty system, for the cameras
alone.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.442
Then recover each point, one at a time.

00:01:29.442 --> 00:01:32.705
This is why large reconstruction is
possible at all.
